Q: How to declare a library class with a method calling a model method in CodeIgniter A: <p>Library classes don't actually need to &quot;extend&quot; the CodeIgniter core controller class. You can just access the &quot;instance&quot; from the constructor of your library.</p>
<p>Whenever you need access to the core instance inside of the library, reference <code>$this-&gt;CI</code></p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;?php
defined('BASEPATH') or exit('No direct script access allowed');

/**
 * @property CI_Controller CI
 */
class CommonLib
{
    public function __construct()
    {
        $this-&gt;CI =&amp; get_instance();
    }

    public function getcountries(): array
    {
        return $this-&gt;CI-&gt;db
            -&gt;order_by('country')
            -&gt;get($this-&gt;countries)
            -&gt;result();
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>I do want to mention that CommonLib seems rather vague for a class name. Libraries are best used to abstract &quot;business logic&quot; away from other layers. For this reason, it is a good idea to silo your libraries into business logic groups.</p>
<p>Libraries should not be directly interacting with the database - that is what model methods are for.</p>
